{
  "dom_id": "step-q80316",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14255260/assets/08d5f10ab1c92cc1dda92728ebbe837a4df43bce572a73fbdfcc5f63ebca90d0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-3,1]\\to [-2,3]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $[-3,1]$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in [-2,3],$ $∃\\,x\\in [-3,1]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ is a surjection",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $3$ and the lowest $y$-value is $-2.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $-2$ and $3$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[-2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of $f$ is $[-2,3]$, it follows that for all $y$ contained within $[-2,3],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-3,1]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is a surjection of $[-3,1]$ onto $[-2,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
